  "notes": [
    "In x86-64, q admits the same integer-register set as r. The frontend normalizes q inputs and outputs to the existing GPR class; no backend, allocator, encoder, IR-schema or optimizer algorithm changes are needed.",
    "The contract supports =q, +q, =&q and +&q for scalar integers through 64 bits and pointers. Uppercase Q and 32-bit q semantics remain outside this subset.",
    "Keel reserves its stack/frame registers; clobbering the other six legacy GPRs forces its byte operands into R8\u2013R10. The native fixture also exercises all scalar widths, pointers, read/write ties and early clobbers.",
    "All 254 prior =q first errors disappear and expose the %a template modifier. This shifts first errors without increasing whole-C-input acceptance.",
    "C remains at four accepted inputs; assembly remains at 402. All 406 prior accepted objects remain byte-for-byte identical, with no crashes, timeouts or acceptance regressions.",
    "First errors can precede IR generation and do not qualify every kernel helper. Constraint alternatives, flag outputs and kernel code-generation contracts remain separate work.",
    "The original corpus and reference artifacts were rehashed, not rebuilt or rebooted. No Keel object was linked into or executed as the kernel."
